Consolidation exercises
Module 4 exercises
The exercises are independent from the adventure and challenge. They use other datasets, either fictitious or public, to consolidate technical skills:

import CSV, Excel and JSON files;
clean types, names and missing values;
recode categories;
manipulate lists and JSON files;
document cleaning decisions in a list.

The series ends with two standalone case studies: last-resort financial assistance in Québec and sports and recreation facilities in Sherbrooke, using public aggregate or municipal data.

Module Objectives
At the end of this module, you should be able to

import data from different formats (csv, Excel, JSON);
inspect types, dimensions, missing values and anomalies;
clean column names, text amounts, factors and character strings;
transform tables with pivot_longer(), pivot_wider() and unnest();
document cleaning decisions in a structured list.

Deliverables
Your GitHub repository must contain the following three files:

defi_04.qmd: your reproducible working document;
donnees_propres.csv: the cleaned dataset;
journal_nettoyage.Rdata: an R object containing the journal_nettoyage list.

Your .qmd file must render to HTML without error.

Minimum Requirements
Your work must include:

a correct import of the file;
an overview of the data structure;
at least five quality checks;
at least three corrections applied to the data;
at least five documented entries in journal_nettoyage;
a saved clean dataset;
a saved journal_nettoyage.

Corrections must be cautious. If an anomaly is real but no obvious correction is defensible, record it in your log or in your text and explain why you do not correct it.
A check can conclude that no change is needed. The five log entries may therefore include justified flags without correction. The three corrections must be distinct decisions actually applied; initializing the log does not count.
